<section id="sec-1">
  <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
  <p>
    Introduced in Java 8, the <code>java.util.stream</code> package fundamentally shifted Java from an imperative, external-iteration model to a declarative, internal-iteration model. At its core, a Stream is a high-level abstraction representing a sequence of elements that supports sequential and parallel aggregate operations. It is <strong>not</strong> a data structure; it stores no elements, does not modify its underlying source, and cannot be reused once consumed.
  </p>
  
  <h3>Enterprise Value Proposition</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Declarative Data Transformation:</strong> Expresses <em>what</em> needs to be accomplished rather than <em>how</em> to iterate, reducing boiler-plate loop mechanics and index-off-by-one errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Lazy Evaluation & Query Optimization:</strong> Stream pipelines defer evaluation until a terminal operation is invoked. Intermediate operations are fused into a single pass, enabling short-circuiting and minimizing redundant memory passes over large datasets.</li>
    <li><strong>Transparent Parallelization:</strong> Abstraxts multi-core parallel execution via the <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code> framework without forcing developers to manually write thread synchronization, data partitioning, or task join code.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common Enterprise Misconceptions & Misuse</h3>
  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <div class="callout-title">Production Warning: High-Throughput Overhead</div>
    Using Streams everywhere blindly is an anti-pattern. Streams incur non-trivial allocation costs (pipeline objects, sink wrappers, lambda captures) and CPU overhead compared to simple primitive <code>for</code> loops. In ultra-low-latency paths (e.g., high-frequency trading or hot-path API routing), streams can trigger severe Garbage Collection pauses and lower throughput due to cache-misses and heap allocation overhead.
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stream $\neq$ Collection:</strong> A Stream is a compute pipeline, not an in-memory data bucket.</li>
    <li><strong>Parallel Streams are not a Silver Bullet for I/O:</strong> Using parallel streams for high-latency external HTTP calls or database queries defaults to the shared <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code>, causing global thread pool starvation across the entire JVM process.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<section id="sec-2">
  <h2>2. Fundamentals & Architecture</h2>
  
  <h3>Core Concepts from First Principles</h3>
  <p>
    A Stream pipeline consists of three distinct phases:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> Collections, Arrays, I/O Channels, Generator Functions, or Primitive Ranges.</li>
    <li><strong>Intermediate Operations (Zero or More):</strong> Operations like <code>map()</code>, <code>filter()</code>, <code>flatMap()</code>, <code>sorted()</code>. These operations transform a stream into another stream and are strictly <em>lazy</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Terminal Operation (Exactly One):</strong> Operations like <code>collect()</code>, <code>reduce()</code>, <code>forEach()</code>, <code>findFirst()</code>. The terminal operation triggers pipeline evaluation, consumes the stream, and yields a result or side-effect.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Stream vs Collection Mechanics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Dimension</th>
        <th>Collection (e.g., <code>List</code>, <code>Set</code>)</th>
        <th>Stream (<code>java.util.stream.Stream</code>)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Data Storage</strong></td>
        <td>In-memory data structure holding explicit data elements.</td>
        <td>No physical storage. Computes elements on demand from a source.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Iteration Model</strong></td>
        <td>External Iteration (Explicit <code>for</code>, <code>Iterator</code> loops).</td>
        <td>Internal Iteration (The pipeline controls traversal).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Evaluation Timing</strong></td>
        <td>Eagerly constructed (All elements reside in memory).</td>
        <td>Lazy evaluation (Evaluated only upon terminal execution).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Reusability</strong></td>
        <td>Can be traversed and mutated infinite times.</td>
        <td>Single-use object. Throws <code>IllegalStateException</code> if reused.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Infinite Sequences</strong></td>
        <td>Impossible (Requires infinite memory).</td>
        <td>Supported via generators (e.g., <code>Stream.iterate()</code>, <code>Stream.generate()</code>).</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

