  <h3>Stream Pipeline Lifecycle</h3>
  <p>
    When a developer writes a stream chain, Java constructs a doubly linked list of pipeline stages extending <code>AbstractPipeline</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>// Construction Phase (No execution occurs)
Stream&lt;String&gt; stream = list.stream()           // Creates Head pipeline stage
    .filter(s -&gt; s.startsWith("A"))             // Wraps Head in StatelessOp stage
    .map(String::toUpperCase);                  // Wraps Filter stage in StatelessOp stage

// Terminal Phase (Pipeline execution begins)
List&lt;String&gt; result = stream.collect(Collectors.toList()); // Triggers Sink chain construction & traversal
</code></pre>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<section id="sec-3">
  <h2>3. Deep Internal Mechanics</h2>

  <h3>1. AbstractPipeline, ReferencePipeline, and Sink Chains</h3>
  <p>
    Internally, streams are managed via internal JDK classes: <code>AbstractPipeline</code> and its subclasses <code>ReferencePipeline</code> (for object streams) and <code>IntPipeline</code>, <code>LongPipeline</code>, <code>DoublePipeline</code> (for primitive specialization).
  </p>
  <p>
    Execution is driven by the <strong><code>Sink&lt;T&gt;</code> interface</strong>. A <code>Sink</code> is a consumer-like interface that receives elements down the stream chain. It contains four core lifecycle operations:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>begin(long size)</code>: Notifies the sink that data is incoming, allowing pre-allocation of buffer sizes.</li>
    <li><code>accept(T t)</code>: Pushes a single data element downstream.</li>
    <li><code>cancellationRequested()</code>: Used by short-circuiting operations (e.g., <code>findFirst</code>, <code>anyMatch</code>) to halt upstream processing.</li>
    <li><code>end()</code>: Signals completion of evaluation. Used by stateful operations (like <code>sorted</code>) to finalize sorting and push buffered results downstream.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <div class="callout-title">Sink Fusing Mechanism</div>
    When a terminal operation executes, the JDK builds a downstream chain of <code>Sink</code> objects in reverse order (from Terminal Op back to Head). As elements are pulled from the <code>Spliterator</code>, they are pushed through the linked <code>Sink.accept()</code> methods without intermediate collection allocations between stages.
  </div>

  <h3>2. Stream Flags (StreamOpFlag)</h3>
  <p>
    The stream framework relies heavily on bitwise flags (<code>StreamOpFlag</code>) to optimize execution paths based on source characteristics and prior intermediate operations.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Flag Name</th>
        <th>Description</th>
        <th>Optimization Impact</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SIZED</code></td>
        <td>Exact size of stream is known.</td>
        <td>Allows collectors (e.g., <code>toList()</code>) to pre-size backing arrays, eliminating dynamic array reallocations.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>DISTINCT</code></td>
        <td>Elements are guaranteed distinct.</td>
        <td>Causes subsequent <code>distinct()</code> calls to become no-ops.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SORTED</code></td>
        <td>Elements are sorted in natural or comparator order.</td>
        <td>Causes subsequent <code>sorted()</code> operations to be bypassed entirely.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ORDERED</code></td>
        <td>Enforces a strict encounter order.</td>
        <td>Disables aggressive parallel optimizations. Calling <code>unordered()</code> clears this flag to increase multi-threaded performance.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SHORT_CIRCUIT</code></td>
        <td>Operation may terminate before processing all inputs.</td>
        <td>Informs execution framework that processing can cease early (e.g., <code>limit()</code>, <code>anyMatch()</code>).</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Spliterator Architecture</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>Spliterator</code> (Splitting Iterator) is the fundamental primitive backing all Streams. It enables sequential and parallel traversal over data sources.
  </p>
  <pre><code>public interface Spliterator&lt;T&gt; {
    boolean tryAdvance(Consumer&lt;? super T&gt; action);
    void forEachRemaining(Consumer&lt;? super T&gt; action);
    Spliterator&lt;T&gt; trySplit();
    long estimateSize();
    int characteristics();
}
</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>tryAdvance()</code>: Processes a single element. Returns <code>true</code> if elements remain, <code>false</code> if exhausted.</li>
    <li><code>trySplit()</code>: The key to parallel processing. Splits the current source into two independent <code>Spliterator</code> instances. If the source cannot be split, it returns <code>null</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Parallel Stream Engine & ForkJoinPool</h3>
  <p>
    When <code>parallelStream()</code> or <code>.parallel()</code> is invoked, execution switches from single-threaded sink execution to a task-decomposing <code>CountedCompleter</code> framework executing within <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="diagram">
Parallel Stream Split & Steal Lifecycle:

                  [ Root Spliterator ]
                           |
                  .trySplit() (Recursive)
                 /                   \
        [ Sub-Spliterator A ]     [ Sub-Spliterator B ]
              /       \                 /       \
          [Task 1]  [Task 2]        [Task 3]  [Task 4]
             |         |               |         |
             v         v               v         v
     --------------------------------------------------
               ForkJoinPool Workers (Work-Stealing)
     --------------------------------------------------
  </div>
</section>

